Scott's Definition 2.2 writes \(u\vdash v\), for consistent finite sets \(u\) and \(v\), to mean that \(u\vdash X\) for every token \(X\) in \(v\). `EntSet` is that quantification over tokens of \(v\). `entSet_empty` proves that every finite set set-entails the empty set, because a token of the empty set cannot exist and the required entailments hold by eliminating that membership. `entSet_singleton` proves that \(u\) set-entails the singleton of a token \(X\) exactly when \(u\) entails \(X\): \(X\) belongs to its own singleton, and every member of that singleton equals \(X\). The definition matches the note, and the two lemmas are the empty-set and singleton cases of the same quantifier. **Theorem:** yes — set-level entailment is `EntSet`. **Proof:** yes — the empty-set argument and the singleton biconditional are the only proofs, and both follow from the quantifier in the definition.
